functionDeclarations.description:
Executes a C# script in the Unity Editor using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Scripting. Captures and returns logs generated during script execution. Supports inline script code or loading from a file path. Two execution modes: (1) one-shot (default, enable_repl=false) — each call is independently compiled and executed, no state shared between calls; (2) REPL session (enable_repl=true) — top-level variable declarations, methods, and type definitions persist across calls within the same session, like typing in a REPL. Session mode exposes the Repl static class in script code: Repl.Id(obj) / Repl.ById(token) for cross-domain-reload object handles, Repl.Vars to inspect session variables, and Repl.ApiHelper for reflection signature queries. In session mode, domain-reload-triggering operations (AssetDatabase.Refresh(), CompilationPipeline.RequestScriptCompilation(), etc.) are blocked by ReplGuard unless unlock_domain_reload=true. Top-level await (await foreach / await using) is rejected; use an inner async local function/lambda and return its Task for async work instead. Blocking calls (Task.Wait, .Result, Thread.Sleep, WaitHandle.WaitOne, etc.) are rejected in both modes. The script must finish quickly and must not block the Unity main thread (see the `script` parameter for exact no-wait/no-blocking constraints). If the script requires the Unity/Tuanjie Editor to be in edit mode (i.e. it relies on AssetDatabase, scene editing, or any operation that is unsafe or unavailable while playing), you MUST first ensure the Editor is NOT in play mode using the unity_editor tool. Call unity_editor with action "get_state" to check whether the Editor is currently playing; if it is, call unity_editor with action "stop", which returns once play mode has actually exited, then re-run "get_state" to confirm it is no longer playing before executing this script. Only invoke "play" if you actually need play mode. If your script modifies an open scene (creating/destroying GameObjects, adding/removing components, or changing any property), you MUST mark the scene dirty and then save it at the end of the script, otherwise the changes are discarded on scene switch:
var scene = UnityEditor.SceneManagement.EditorSceneManager.GetActiveScene();
UnityEditor.SceneManagement.EditorSceneManager.MarkSceneDirty(scene);
UnityEditor.SceneManagement.EditorSceneManager.SaveScene(scene);

script.description:
C# script code to execute OR absolute path to a .cs file. If a file path is provided (e.g., "/path/to/script.cs"), Unity will automatically detect it and read the file content. Make sure the passed in path is valid utf-8 encoded path. PREFERRED for executing existing script files. The script runs in the Unity Editor C# environment with access to UnityEngine and UnityEditor namespaces. When you need to capture a screenshot of the gameview during the execution of the script, use following methods
namespace UnityTcp.Editor.Tools
{
    public static class ManageScreenshot
    {
        public static bool BeginCapture(float scale = 1f); // Starts a new capture session and immediately grabs the first Game View frame into memory.
        public static bool CaptureFrame(); // Captures one more Game View frame into the active session's in-memory buffer.
        public static string EndCaptureToGif(string filePath, int fps = 25, int colorCount = 128); // Ends the active session, encodes all buffered frames into an animated .gif, writes it to disk.
    }
}
This is an immediate Editor automation script: it MUST finish quickly and MUST NOT wait. Never emit blocking calls: no Thread.Sleep, Thread.SpinWait, Task.Wait, Task.Result, .WaitOne() (on AutoResetEvent/ManualResetEvent/Mutex/Semaphore), and no busy-wait loops (e.g. while(cond){} with no yield) — all of these freeze the Unity main thread. Do NOT implement any delay, polling, or frame-spanning wait inside this one-off script (it is a Roslyn script, not a coroutine/IEnumerator body, so yield return / WaitForSeconds / WaitUntil do not apply here). If you need multi-frame work, a delay, polling, or a long-running task, split it into Unity builtin request/wait tools (e.g. request_* -> wait_*), repeat tool calls, or create a proper Unity script with lifecycle code instead.
